1
00:00:00,250 --> 00:00:04,317
Hello! Last time we learned about vowels.

2
00:00:04,317 --> 00:00:09,493
Today, we'll meet another main character of Hangeul, the consonants.

3
00:00:09,493 --> 00:00:13,930
What shapes were Hangeul consonants modeled after?

4
00:00:14,630 --> 00:00:19,720
Surprisingly, Hangeul consonants were modeled after the shapes of our bodies that make sounds,

5
00:00:19,720 --> 00:00:29,290
namely, the mouth, tongue, and throat. This is called the 'principle of pictography'.

6
00:00:29,990 --> 00:00:38,961
First, "ㄱ" (giyeok). It's the shape of the root of the tongue blocking the throat. If you make the sound, it's 'geu'.

7
00:00:38,961 --> 00:00:42,900
It's a sound made near the molars.

8
00:00:43,600 --> 00:00:53,180
"ㄴ" (nieun) is the shape of the tongue tip touching the upper gums. 'neu'. The tongue slightly touches the front of the palate.

9
00:00:53,880 --> 00:01:02,830
"ㅁ" (mieum) is the shape of tightly closed lips. 'meu'. It's a sound where both lips meet and close.

10
00:01:03,530 --> 00:01:12,790
"ㅅ" (siot) is the shape of pointed teeth. 'seu'. It's a sound where air leaks between the teeth.

11
00:01:13,490 --> 00:01:22,106
"ㅇ" (ieung) is the shape of a rounded throat. When used as a final consonant, it becomes the nasal sound 'eung'.

12
00:01:22,106 --> 00:01:27,360
In the initial consonant position, it's a silent placeholder.

13
00:01:28,060 --> 00:01:35,551
These five basic consonants, "ㄱ", "ㄴ", "ㅁ", "ㅅ",

14
00:01:35,551 --> 00:01:39,920
and "ㅇ" were born by imitating our bodies.

15
00:01:40,620 --> 00:01:46,442
What about the rest of the consonants? A very clever method was used.

16
00:01:46,442 --> 00:01:56,630
When the sound becomes stronger, one stroke is added to the basic letter. This is called the 'principle of adding strokes'.

17
00:01:57,330 --> 00:02:04,985
Adding a stroke to "ㄴ" makes "ㄷ" (digeut), and to "ㅁ" makes "ㅂ" (bieup).

18
00:02:04,985 --> 00:02:09,160
The sounds become much clearer and firmer.

19
00:02:09,860 --> 00:02:18,319
Adding a stroke to "ㅅ" makes "ㅈ" (jieut), and to "ㅇ" makes "ㅎ" (hieut).

20
00:02:18,319 --> 00:02:25,240
And even "ㄹ" (rieul) which rolls the tongue. Do you see the pattern?

21
00:02:25,940 --> 00:02:30,111
Here are the ten basic consonants we'll learn today.

22
00:02:30,111 --> 00:02:40,867
"ㄱ", "ㄴ", "ㄷ", "ㄹ", "ㅁ", "ㅂ", "ㅅ", "ㅇ", "ㅈ", "ㅎ".

23
00:02:40,867 --> 00:02:43,940
Shall we call their names together?

24
00:02:44,640 --> 00:02:47,353
Now, the most amazing part!

25
00:02:47,353 --> 00:02:55,701
Hangeul does not write consonants and vowels separately; it combines them into one character.

26
00:02:55,701 --> 00:02:59,040
This is called 'combined writing'.

27
00:02:59,740 --> 00:03:12,201
The consonant is the initial sound, the vowel is the middle sound. If we combine initial sound "ㄱ" and middle sound "ㅏ"?

28
00:03:12,201 --> 00:03:15,600
It becomes '가' (ga).

29
00:03:16,300 --> 00:03:28,746
The rule is simple. Vowels with vertical lines like 'ㅏ, ㅓ, ㅣ' attach to the right of the consonant.

30
00:03:28,746 --> 00:03:32,140
Like na, meo, di.

31
00:03:32,840 --> 00:03:43,222
Conversely, vowels with horizontal lines like 'ㅗ, ㅜ, ㅡ' attach below the consonant.

32
00:03:43,222 --> 00:03:46,500
Like go, nu, deu.

33
00:03:47,200 --> 00:03:50,833
Today we'll only learn letters without a final consonant.

34
00:03:50,833 --> 00:03:58,100
Without a final consonant, the sound opens up clearly, which is called an 'open syllable'.

35
00:03:58,800 --> 00:04:07,427
Now, shall we make words? If we write '고' (go) and '기' (gi) next to each other, it's '고기' (gogi).

36
00:04:07,427 --> 00:04:11,740
Two letters combined to create meaning!

37
00:04:12,440 --> 00:04:23,503
Shall we call out family members? '나' (na) with 'ㄴ' and 'ㅏ', and then '누나' (nuna).

38
00:04:23,503 --> 00:04:32,670
'오빠' (oppa) and '누나' (nuna) are affectionate words to call family.

39
00:04:33,370 --> 00:04:50,940
'ㅁ, ㅓ, ㄹ, ㅣ' makes '머리' (meori). 'ㅂ, ㅏ, ㄴ, ㅏ, ㄴ, ㅏ' makes '바나나' (banana).

40
00:04:50,940 --> 00:04:56,670
Consonants and vowels combine perfectly, don't they?

41
00:04:57,370 --> 00:05:07,110
'아버지' (abeoji) and '어머니' (eomeoni). Even with only letters without final consonants, we can write such warm words.

42
00:05:07,810 --> 00:05:15,797
'오이' (oi), '호수' (hosu), '지도' (jido). These are all easy words without final consonants.

43
00:05:15,797 --> 00:05:19,160
Read along out loud.

44
00:05:19,860 --> 00:05:27,518
Now, a flashcard game! '고기' (gogi), '호수' (hosu), '기차' (gicha).

45
00:05:27,518 --> 00:05:33,320
Read them clearly, recalling the consonants and vowels.

46
00:05:34,020 --> 00:05:38,927
It's also good to write the learned consonants stroke by stroke with a pencil.

47
00:05:38,927 --> 00:05:42,680
Writing by hand helps remember the shapes and sounds better.

48
00:05:43,380 --> 00:05:49,890
Today we learned ten basic consonants and combined writing, where consonants and vowels are grouped together.

49
00:05:49,890 --> 00:05:53,920
Now you too can read and write simple words!

50
00:05:54,620 --> 00:06:02,623
If you practice a little every day, your Hangeul skills will grow quickly. Let's continue our Hangeul journey together with a subscription and a like.

51
00:06:02,623 --> 00:06:04,290
See you next time!
